---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE, SITE_DESCRIPTION } from "../consts";
//import react from "@astrojs/react";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
		<link
			href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.0.2/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css"
			rel="stylesheet"
			integrity="sha384-EVSTQN3/azprG1Anm3QDgpJLIm9Nao0Yz1ztcQTwFspd3yD65VohhpuuCOmLASjC"
			crossorigin="anonymous"
		/>
		<script src="https://cdn.plot.ly/plotly-latest.min.js"></script>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			
			<div
				style="display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center;"
			>
				<h1>CHSH Tinkerer</h1><br />
			</div>

			<div
				style="display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center;"
			>
				<h2>Quantum Entanglement<br />Strategy Tinkerer</h2>
			</div>
			<p>
				&vert;A<sub>0</sub>&rangle; = cos(&alpha;)&vert;0&rangle; +
				sin(&alpha;)&vert;1&rangle; and &vert;B<sub>0</sub>&rangle; =
				cos(&beta;)&vert;0&rangle; + sin(&beta;)&vert;1&rangle;
			</p>
			<script src="../../src/components/quantumPage2script.js"></script>
			<div id="qpage2chart1"></div>
			<label for="SelectorForGraph1">Options:</label>
			<select class="form-select form-select d-inline-block mb-3 w-25" id="SelectorForGraph1" name="SelectorForGraph1">
				<option value="0">x = 0, y = 0</option>
				<option value="1">x = 0, y = 1</option>
				<option value="2">x = 1, y = 0</option>
				<option value="3">x = 1, y = 1</option>
			</select>
			<button id="reset">Reset</button>

			<div>
				<label
					>Alpha angle for &vert;A<sub>0</sub>&rangle; (degrees):</label
				>
				<input
					id="alphaAnglePicker"
					class="form-control"
					type="number"
					value="0"
				/>

				<br />
				<label
					>Alpha angle for &vert;A<sub>1</sub>&rangle; (degrees):</label
				>
				<input
					id="alphaAnglePicker2"
					class="form-control"
					type="number"
					value="45"
				/>
				<br />
				<label
					>Beta angle for &vert;B<sub>0</sub>&rangle; (degrees):</label
				>
				<input
					id="betaAnglePicker"
					class="form-control"
					type="number"
					value="22.5"
				/>
				<br />
				<label
					>Beta angle for &vert;B<sub>1</sub>&rangle; (degrees):</label
				>
				<input
					id="betaAnglePicker2"
					class="form-control"
					type="number"
					value="-22.5"
				/>
				<br />
				<span
					>Difference in angle between &vert;A<sub>0</sub>&rangle; and
					&vert;B<sub>0</sub>&rangle;:
				</span><span id="diffInAngleText">22.5</span><span>
					degrees</span
				>
				<br />
				<span>Win Percentage: </span><span id="winPercenttext"
					>85.35533905932738</span
				><span> %</span>
				<br>
				<br>
				<p>
					Conclusion:
					<br>
					No matter what option you choose, the highest winning percent is about 85.36%. If you analyze the results of trying different numbers in this tinkerer, you will find that one strategy prevails: 
					Keeping the angle between &vert;A<sub>0</sub>&rangle; and
					&vert;B<sub>0</sub>&rangle; at 22.5 degrees or &#x3C0;/8 radians away from each other. &vert;A<sub>0</sub>&rangle; and
					&vert;A<sub>1</sub>&rangle; should be 45 degrees away. On the same note, &vert;B<sub>0</sub>&rangle; and
					&vert;B<sub>1</sub>&rangle; should be 45 degrees away. The only way to get the maximum winning percent is to do a derivate of this strategy. 
					<br>
					This proves that quantum entanglement is useful in real problems. We can get better results if we apply the benefits of quantum entagled bits in the correct way. 

				</p>
			</div>
		</main>

		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
